@@ -0,0 +1,37 @@
+# Contentful paint timing
+
+`paint-timing.js` starts automatically on the page. It measures contentful updates
+caused by interactions, including asynchronous work, using the browser's
+`interaction-contentful-paint` entries. All durations are in milliseconds.
+
+Read `window.contentfulPaintTiming.support` for browser capability flags and
+`window.contentfulPaintTiming.getReports()` for the latest 100 reports. Subscribe
+to live measurements with:
+
+```js
+window.addEventListener('contentful-paint-metric', ({ detail }) => {
+  console.log(detail);
+});
+```
+
+Interaction reports include the initiating interaction ID, start time, elapsed
+duration to presentation, and content size. These are increasing contentful paint
+candidates, so an interaction can produce several reports; they are not INP.
+
+Soft-navigation reports include navigation/interaction IDs, FCP duration, and the
+largest contentful paint's duration and size. They finalize on the next soft
+navigation, when the document becomes hidden, on pagehide, or on `disconnect()`.
+The navigation's initial candidate includes content painted before its URL update;
+later candidates match by interaction ID. Timestamps use the original document's
+time origin; LCP duration is relative to the soft navigation's start.
+
+This page currently uses ordinary document links, not a client-side router. Such
+links do not emit soft-navigation reports. No artificial timing estimates are
+produced for unsupported browsers or interactions without new contentful paints.
+Observers are buffered and feature-detected independently; no analytics network
+requests are made and reports retain no DOM elements, text, or URLs. This adds no
+replacement for standard hard-navigation metrics.
+
+Call `window.contentfulPaintTiming.disconnect()` to flush pending entries and remove
+the observers and lifecycle listeners. Run `npm test` for Node-based observer and
+lifecycle checks; these tests need no browser or server.
